A||||25||lowCG||Blowers?
Z||000000||lowCG||07-09-99||11:51 AM||||Has anyone seen,or experienced the roots-type blower on the 60DEG V6?<br>The Eaton unit has the inlet right where the throttle body is,stock,and the stock manifold base appears to be quite similar to those sold as special blower units.
Z||000001||theogre||07-09-99||09:51 PM||||Never put a roots blower on a steet car. The first time the car backfires the roots could go right thru the deck lid. Positive Displacement air pumps, like "roots" blowers, have a number of design and operational problems. These problems regularly manifest themselves in very ugly ways. Roots units are great on drag cars that are torn down constantly, even then NHRA/IHRA have very strict rules how they are installed. There is a very good reason both sanctioning bodies require blowouts in the manifold. Those blowout panels keep the whole blower/manifold from flying off the car.
Z||000002||Racer 1||07-09-99||10:46 PM||||I agree with The Orge a "Hot blower" landing on your head isnt fun!back in the old drag strip days this was a regular affair untill the mandatory saftey straps for blowers were ammended!!
Z||000003||PaUL||07-10-99||02:04 AM||||What kind of supercharger do production cars use? Its my understanding that the roots type blower was modeled on a wheat threshing machine and works like an oil pump only it pumps air instead of oil. I thought most blowers were roots type, other than volkswagons G ladder blower used on the shortlived Corrado, what kind of blowers are there?
Z||000004||lowCG||07-10-99||03:52 PM||||Well I bet grandpa and grandma would be surprised if the blower in their stock 3.8,GM cruiser were to explode,might even have to pull the chute early! Same for Chip and Muffy in the Mercedes convertible,they really hate it when their blower flies off,ruins the picnic.<br>But really if you consider that when not running a top fuel car,things are a little less stressed,clearanced looser and are actually quite reliable;no 14:1 compression,no 28 PSI boost,no methanol/nitro,just 8-10 PSI,9:1 compression,and maybe 260HP,not 1200+HP.
Z||000005||PaUL||07-10-99||04:17 PM||||you tell em lowCG, that ogre thinks he know everything
Z||000006||ezsurf||07-10-99||05:13 PM||||The type of blower more production cars are going to today is the corkscrew type.  Whipple makes them, and I believe Eaton does as well.  They are more reliable and put less wear and tear on internal components.  The blower in the Pontiac 3.8l engine is a corkscrew type.  Instead of compressing air vertically, it compresses air into a pie shaped area at one end of the supercharger.  I already checked to see if Whipple makes one for the 2.8l, they don't.  I don't know about Eaton.
Z||000007||PaUL||07-10-99||05:35 PM||||yeah, Ive seen the screw type. Its like 2 screws meshing together. This is another example of old, ancient in this case, technology in use today. Archimedes came up with this idea to pump water thousands of years ago.
Z||000008||batboy||07-10-99||11:00 PM||||I think one of those Pontiac Grand Prix V6 3800 supercharged engines would be nice sitting in a Fiero. I'd love to drive one.
