Z||000010||theogre||07-11-99||05:00 PM||||the one that looks like 2 meshed screws is still a positive displasment unit. I don't know exactly what one you saw Paul, but it's likely a variant of a roots family unit. EZ, If I have your description right, you are describing the "half turbine" type blowers made by Paxton and Vortec. These are basically the intake side of a trubo chrager which is gear driven off the fan belt.<p>Most production cars have used a turbo charger. There are a couple reasons for this. 1 is that trubines use waste energy to produce the boost. 2 is that turbines are very reliable, and easily tolerate a backfire in the intakes. Belt Driven half turbines, like Paxton and Vortec units, share both these features.<p>Positive displacement units like Roots blowers do not tolerate an intake backfire well at all. Roots and piston pumps have a "closed" compressor path and without a blow out pannel there is no place for a backfire's pressure wave to escape. In a turbine, which is basically a fan spinning really fast, the pressure pulse just blows back thru the rather open impeller path. A positive displacement Roots trys to compress the backfire pulse, which it can't do, and something has to give. Usually what gives is very expensive unless a blow out pannel is installed. That I know of there is very few classes of racing that allows Roots blower without some type of pressure release. This release is installed entirly for saftey reasons in most cases. This in contrast to Turbine applications where bost limiters are installed, nearly exclusively, to level out the playing field.<p>Some new production cars may indeed use a roots blower, but I expect they are "sloppy" blowers, or that there is some type of safety device to release backfire impulses. A "sloppy" blower has allot of internal clearance. That should allow it to survive the occasional back fire, but limmits it's effeciency tremendously.<p>In Fiero the design of the V6 intake and exhaust makes turbo charging very easy. There is even plenty of room over the tranny for it. Blowers on the other hand require a belt feed. There just isn't a whole lot of room for that in Fiero w/o making major changes someplace. You might could stuff a half turbo blower in the area where the battery sits, but that of course requires moving the bat up front. Bracketing, and plumbing the thing would be a giant bitch.
Z||000011||ezsurf||07-11-99||06:24 PM||||Ogre,<br>I am familiar with the Paxton and Vortec belt driven turbochargers.  I am not refering to them.  The Whipple web site is still under construction, but Eaton's web site has the type I'm refering to.  Eaton calls it a modified roots type.  I would say that it is probably a positive displacement unit.  I know the blowout panels you are refering to, and I see them on every dragster I've ever seen.  However, I don't believe blowouts are such a problem for superchargers on street machines.  I'm sure they're not turning out nearly the same amount of boost where the problem really comes into play.  B & M has been making a low profile blower unit for engines like a 350 Chevy for years.  To my knowledge this unit doesn't have a blowout panel.  But, then again I've never examined one specifically looking for a panel.
Z||000012||theogre||07-11-99||06:40 PM||||I've seen the B&M mini roots. no it doesn't have a blowout as I remember.<p>If you are going to use a roots, the motor must be in very good shape. You also have to follow a few rules. #1 is make sure everything is up to temp before you romp on it. Even the best motor can burp when it's cold. Even if the motor doesn't come appart when it burps, you will do some damage. To many burps,and something breaks.<p>I won't deni allot of people opt for Roots blowers. I don't like them on street cars for the reasons above. While turbochargers have problems with cooking out bearings, allot of which is fixed by adding an oil accumulator, they don't cause the trouble  Roots blowers do. I haven't seen the ones Ez mentions. I'll have to look into them more.
Z||000013||Eric||07-11-99||08:58 PM||||I never thought the turbo would be the "best" option because the the long exaust path to the y pipe.  Wouldn't this create a greater amount of lag?  I know there are many V6s with this setup, but I guess these aren't the same people who race thier cars too.
Z||000014||ezsurf||07-11-99||09:16 PM||||Personally I like turbochargers myself.  The addition of a turbo oiler to a turbocharger usually takes care of burnt bearings.  The problem comes from the amount of boost you run.  If the boost is low enough, it's not a problem.  If you run too much boost you get into spark knock (detonation), unless the engine has a knock sensor to electronically retard the timing to prevent damaging the engine.  The 87 Buick Grand national had such a sensor.  Other model years did not.  This contributed to the unreliability of the engine set up.  I don't know if any turbo setups on the Fiero are equipted with an anti knock sensor.  Also, if your going to run a lot of boost, you need internal engine components that will take the stress.  Otherwise, your just going to burn it up anyway.
Z||000015||theogre||07-11-99||10:37 PM||||MSD has anti knock items to add to a motor. I don't know how much use they are for racing.<p>Fiero v6 has about the shortest Y pipe posibility I know of. You can replace it and the down pipe fairly simply and install the turbine in between. The turbine then sits right on top the tranny ramming air into the factory plennum. You can even retain the factory air cleaner can. (Yes, you'll likely have to make the pipes.)<p>With the settups I've seen, the only turbo lag you'll get in Fiero is caused by inertia, not by lenght of pipes. Inertial lag is directly related to the design of the turbo, not the plumbing.<p>If you do use a trubo, remember to move all the vacuum operated things to the intake side of the turbo. If you leave them connected to the original location, they won't work right. This includes the Power Brake booster!
Z||000016||lowCG||07-11-99||11:20 PM||||Yeah,rotor design has improved a bunch,allowing a "sloppy" blower to perform very well.I'm not a big fan of the turbine compressors.I'm wondering how much a new unit costs,the kits for other vehicles are around $3K,so I'm guessing the units are $1200-$1500 maybe?
