Z||000018||Eric||07-12-99||03:07 PM||||Okay, turbos are good for the 2.8.  My question is about the pistons.  Would forged pistons be needed to hold up under the pressure of say 10lbs. of boost or could you get away with a cheaper hypereutectic?  What compression would be best for the a maximum of 10lbs of boost?  I was considering installing a turbo on my 3.2 (when I build it) but changed my mind when I was told I had to go with a low compression forged piston to hold up under turbo applications.  That may be alright if there were those type of pistons available and I was going to install the turbo right away.
Z||000019||ezsurf||07-12-99||07:26 PM||||Most turbo engines don't have a compression ratio over about 9:1.  I'm not sure what type of pistons to use, but turbo engines usually have a reinforced crank also.  Some turbo systems require water injection for cooling as well.  It certainly doesn't hurt.  If you have a system with an intercooler it saves a lot of stress on the engine.  I would talk to a turbo manufacturer like Air Research or Garrett, and see what they recommend.  Also take a look at a dedicated turbo engine and see what it has on it. i.e. dish pistons, special gaskets, heat shields, etc. 
Z||000020||PaUL||07-12-99||07:37 PM||||I thought most modern production turbo engines use some kind of knock sensor intead of water injection. When the detonation starts to occur, the computer retards the timing.
Z||000021||theogre||07-12-99||07:40 PM||||On ECM's that can do that, yes. Knock sensor is used on allot of motors now.... they are running them so lean for emmisions that they ping really easy.
Z||000022||Cooter||07-12-99||08:58 PM||||From what I have read, the water injection on turbo motors helps keep down detonation and helps allow higher static compression for better performance in off boost conditions. I also heard it is pretty cool to blend methanol (or nitromethane) in the injection unit. That sounds fun! Go to the hobby shop and pick up a gallon of 45% nitro model airplane fuel. It smells really neat burning-castor oil, methanol and nitromethane has a unique odor. Unfortunatly, it aint legal in "stock" kart racing. But that is another story altogether. . . 
